.TH MDT 1 "2026-10-09" "mdt 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
.nf
    mdt — read, compare and edit saved Markdown

.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
    mdt read FILE [selection] [--rev REV] [output options]
    mdt diff FILE --since REV [--rev REV] [--node LABEL]... [output options]
    mdt find FILE TEXT [--node LABEL]... [--rev REV] [output options]
    mdt outline FILE [--node LABEL]... [--rev REV] [output options]
    mdt replace FILE --rev REV [--node LABEL]... --old TEXT --new TEXT [write options]
    mdt replace FILE --rev REV selection --body FILE|- [write options]
    mdt insert FILE --rev REV (--before TARGET|--after TARGET) --body FILE|-
        [write options]
    mdt history FILE [output options]
    mdt forget FILE [--dry-run] [--max-bytes N] [--json]
    mdt COMMAND FILE --cursor CURSOR    (read, diff, find, outline, history)

.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
    Operate on saved Markdown without starting Emm. Source remains ordinary
    Markdown. Versions, targets and continuations live in bounded local storage.
    No command records an agent's identity or advances a reading position.

    Each command does one operation. read returns text; diff compares versions;
    find locates exact source; outline lists structure. replace and insert write.
    history lists retained observations; forget removes retained versions.

.SH VERSIONS
.nf
    --rev selects the version against which selections and edit intentions are
    interpreted. Without it, reads use current saved text. Writes require it.
    --since is the start of a diff; --rev is its endpoint. Both are revisions.
    A comparison reports net differences. A → B → A has no net difference;
    retained history may show the intervening observations.

    Versions identify bytes, not what anyone has read. Keep the earlier baseline
    until the desired comparison has been delivered and processed. Reading one
    section establishes no knowledge of its siblings. Observation dates are not
    guaranteed edit dates. Unobserved intermediate saves may be absent.

.SH SELECTION
.nf
    --node matches exact heading/list-item labels. Repeat labels for ancestry.
    --target selects an exact range returned at --rev. D is the document range.
    These are source locations, not permanent identities embedded in Markdown.
    Named diff selection checks both sides so removed sections remain discoverable.
    Duplicate or missing selections are explicit errors, never guessed matches.

.SH CONTEXT AND OUTPUT
.nf
    Short prose may be shown as a complete paragraph or comment. Long prose uses
    exact before/current excerpts. Heading paths and explicit comment stamps aid
    interpretation. Source-line length does not determine the output bound.
    diff and find accept --context-words N with the same meaning.

    --max-bytes caps the complete UTF-8 response, including metadata; default 8192.
    Reads, diffs, search, outline and history use complete and next_cursor when
    paging. --cursor continues that same query at pinned versions and options.
    Conflicting options on a continuation are a usage error.
    Pending changed source is never silently omitted as if it were merely context.

    Data is written to stdout. Errors and plain-text notices go to stderr.
    --json returns structured data with exact source strings and embedded result
    notices. Errors in JSON mode go to stderr. No automatic pager or prompts.

.SH WRITES
.nf
    --body accepts a source file or - for stdin; --old/--new accept literals.
    Input is exact, including whitespace and the final newline. There is no
    implicit dedentation, list renumbering or newline stripping.
    Literal replacement requires exactly one match. Empty replacement deletes.
    Counts describe affected occurrences or targets, not characters or lines.

    Resolve the intended target at --rev first. For a literal edit, the target is
    the matched range, not its entire containing section. Changes elsewhere in
    that section can therefore coexist with the edit. If the range cannot be
    located unambiguously in current source, refuse. A newly duplicated literal
    does not authorize choosing the first match.

    An unchanged, unambiguous target can be edited while other parts changed.
    The receipt gives previous and resulting versions and a bounded notice of
    intervening edits. The same diff command can inspect them.
    A changed target is refused unless --force explicitly permits its replacement
    or permits insertion beside a changed, still-unambiguous anchor.
    --force does not disable unique selection or exact literal matching.

.\" Reporting revision: Codex, GPT-6 (exact variant unavailable), session 01a15578-ff67-77e2-90a9-ffccd1c679b3, 2026-10-19.
    If a valid baseline target is missing or ambiguous in changed source, the
    error reports that change and its retained current_revision. Exit codes stay
    3 and 4. Invalid baseline selections remain ordinary selection errors.
    Plain-text and JSON errors show current_revision when available. Inspect it
    with read --rev REV or diff --since BASE --rev REV.

    Preserve a changed pre-write version before committing, so forced overwrites
    remain inspectable within retention. Refuse the concurrent write if that
    prerequisite cannot be met. Check and commit against one consistent live view.
    Preserve source outside the selected edit; never apply a partial write.

    If the final file check observes different bytes, refuse the write (exit 9)
    and retain that exact version as current_revision. If retention fails, report
    that failure without a revision. No source write committed in either case.
    The reported version may already have been superseded by another save.
    Writers ignoring the shared gate can still race the final read and rename.
    Unobserved changes cannot be detected or retained; writer identity is unknown.

    --dry-run validates and reports the same operation without mutating Markdown
    (or retention records for forget). It reserves nothing. A lost receipt is an
    unknown result; inspect current state before retrying an insertion.

.SH RETENTION
.nf
    History, versions and references are local and bounded, not a backup promise.
    Expiry produces revision_unavailable rather than guessed continuity.
    forget removes retained state for a file, never its source Markdown.
    A normal read can establish a new retained view after expiry.

.SH FILES
.nf
    ~/.emm/notes/updates.sqlite3
        Unified store for observed versions and retained references,
        shared with Emm Notes. There is no separate CLI snapshot cache to manage.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.nf
    EMM_HOME
        Replaces ~/.emm; state is in its notes subdirectory. Relative values are
        resolved from the working directory; an empty value means that directory.
        Use the same resolved location in Emm and mdt to share observations.
    QM_STATE_DIR
        Overrides the CLI state location with an absolute directory for isolated
        use. It does not relocate Emm Notes' state. Empty or relative values are
        refused. Use isolated note copies: writers using different state stores
        do not share write coordination.

.SH EXIT STATUS
.nf
    0  Success, including no changes, a no-op or a find with no matches.
    2  Invalid arguments or a requested budget too small for a result envelope.
    3  File, selection or literal replacement match not found.
    4  Ambiguous edit or selection.
    5  The target changed; no write committed.
    6  A required retained version or reference is unavailable.
    9  Write contention; no write committed.
   10  Storage or I/O failure. A receipt must distinguish a successful source save
       followed by a retention warning from a save that never happened.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
    mdt read note.md
    mdt diff note.md --since R1
    mdt diff note.md --since R1 --rev R2
    mdt find note.md 'the old wording'
    mdt replace note.md --rev R2 --old 'the old wording' --new 'the new wording'
    mdt insert note.md --rev R2 --before D --body introduction.md
    mdt history note.md

.SH SEE ALSO
.nf
    mdt COMMAND -h
    mdt feedback -h
